          "severity": "MAJOR",
          "category": "distractor_ambiguity",
          "description": "Distractor B ('for pedestrians and cyclists alike more accessible') is grammatically parseable as correct under an alternate bracketing: 'the downtown corridor for pedestrians and cyclists alike' can be read as a single complex object noun phrase (postnominal PP modifying 'corridor', similar to 'the room for guests'), with 'more accessible' as the causative object complement — i.e. 'will make [the downtown corridor for pedestrians and cyclists alike] [more accessible]'. Under that (marked but grammatical) reading the sentence is fully coherent, making B MARGINAL rather than cleanly INVALID. This is in tension with Hard Rule §7.2.3 ('no distractor may be correct under any plausible reading'). D remains the clearly intended and more natural answer, and most test-takers would reject B on first reading, so this falls short of a full two-valid-answers critical failure, but it is a real design weakness.",
          "related_check": "answer_uniqueness"
        }
      ],
      "revision_requirements": [
